ce  um after brexit so if you uh click on  the i that will  take you to that and another film that  we we have made  is looking at how the eu has changed  since the brexit vote  um and um fabian cameron has sent in  this question  do you think any of european uh of any  of the european countries may benefit  from brexit  and if so which one i'm not sure  i think brexit is basically not a very  good thing for the european union any  more than it is for  for the uk in economic terms but it is  certainly true that places like the  netherlands and france  have gained some company headquarters  companies  who've moved out of out of the uk to con  to the continent  france seems to have gained a little bit  of  financial business that that has shifted  not much but a little bit has shifted  from  out of london which continues to be the  biggest financial center but certainly  some continental centers are trying to  trying to take some of that that  business themselves  um ireland and dublin has also managed  to  get quite a lot of fund managers to move  funds over to over to  um to the republic so there are a few  sectors where i think people feel  they've done slightly better  as a result of the brexit vote than they  would otherwise have done  um let's just move on now to the sort of  looking at the long-term implications  of brexit um we had quite a lot of  questions they were asking about whether  um britain might become a tax haven um  i know that at some point there was a  suggestion that britain might become  like singapore for example  what do you think the chances are of  that actually happening  well i think that's going to be a  subject that will be very heavily  discussed in future negotiations  um what the european union has tried to  do during the last sort of year and a  half of negotiating over brexit  is to say that we would expect the uk  as a very close um country that's close  to us  that is also quite competitive we would  expect them to  stick to most of the rules that they  play that they apply today  but many people in the pro-brexit arm of  the tory party want to actually tear up  quite a lot of these regulations  the eu's response to that is likely to  be to say if you do that  and you go down a road of deregulating  more reducing labor standards reducing  environmental standards  we won't offer you such a generous free  trade deal we'll limit your access to  our markets because they don't they do  fear  a low tax low regulation competitor on  their doorstep  race to the bottom as people are calling  out um  we've got a question here from from bob  uh dunkley  asking what sort of deals could the uk  make with other nations to  to boost their boost their economy after  brexit  well there will be and there certainly  will be an attempt by  if it's the present government or even  the future government to do free trade  deals with with third countries  um uh and obviously people talk  particularly about a free trade deal  with the united states or with china or  with india  and i think that those those talks will  go ahead  um how fast that will be is a very  difficult question  um because experience suggests it takes  a long time to do a free trade deal  they don't usually come for free and  they don't cover all trades how long  does that how long did canada's take  canada's free trade deal with the with  the eu took nine years  um and on top of that it's difficult to  see  free to see it being easy to do a free  trade deal for example is the united  states in particular  because the americans tend to lay down  all the rules that they want to observe  and they don't  take any rules from anybody else and the  mood of actually both parties in america  both the republicans and the democrats  it's not exactly pro-free trade at the  moment  so i think that the gains that are out  there for potential  free trade deals with third countries  are much smaller than some of the  pro-brexit  people claim and what impact  will that have on on our economy after  brexit if  if if it takes a very long time to  negotiate these trade deals  well most of the numbers that have  looked at this suggest that the impact  on the economy of  further free trade deals even with  countries like america china and india  would be quite small  mainly because around half of our trade  britain's trade is with the european  union that is declining slowly  but it's still by far the biggest  trading partner we've got  and if you've introduced as brexit will  do  more friction and more barriers in the  trade relationship with the european  union  the losses you make from that are going  to be far bigger than any gains you can  get from free trade deals with third  countries for quite a long time to come  um i should say this point uh we've got  a lot more people joining so  uh hello to all of you and uh thank you  for joining us please do send in any  questions you might have on brexit  joined by john pete who is the brexit  editor here at the economist  who is answering many of your questions  and and will continue to answer any more  that you send in  and we've got a question here from  maniac singh um  asking about whether brexit will affect  the ongoing u.s china trade war  um i don't think it will have a direct  effect because that's  that is very much a sort of bilateral um  between donald trump and and and the  chinese um  but brexit is one of the factors that is  seen as sort of not benefiting the world  economy at the moment  like the u.s trying to trade war so i  think it plays into what looks like a  sort of slightly gloomier  scenario for the world economy i mean  the imf keeps  down reducing its predictions of growth  and what brexit is one of the reasons  for that  and if we if we do head into recession  if there is a global recession  and and we leave with a deal what will  that impact be  will there you know as opposed to if  there isn't you know there's just a  downturn well in a way i think it may  mean that we  we end up implementing brexit at a time  which is what maximum economic  uncertainty  um and therefore the impact will will  not be helpful  do you think there is an argument to if  even if we are going to leave to deal  to delay it to wait for the global  economy to  no i don't because i think the politics  will trump that but i mean it is true  that it would be it would be better to  do something like this at a time when  the world economy is growing  and the european economy is growing but  it looks at the moment if the world  economy is slowing and the european  economy  is tipping on the on the edge of  recession we've got time just for one  more question  and this one comes from major bison  will europeans apply for uk visas and  vice versa going forward  well the government has said that they  will end free movement of people  which means the the right of any  european union citizen to  to come and live and work here so they  will introduce a new regime  which would mean that if you wanted to  come and work here from continental  europe you may well have to apply for  some kind of work permit or visa  it won't affect normal tourism or normal  travel because  rather as between most eu countries in  the united states you can do that  with some other in some other way some  electronic way of doing it  so i think people will still be  traveling but it will certainly affect  the world of work  thank you thank you very much and we're  going to leave it there  uh thank you so much to everyone who's  joined us thank you for sending in your  questions  and we're going to put this video up on  our channel so you'll be able to watch  the  the whole q a session with john  um later on today we will be back with  another live q a  at some point hopefully next week um  thank you very much for joining us  and it's going to be an interesting week  it's a busy week a busy week thank you  bye
 

